<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.berwilson.cron-pepper-reply</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/zsh</string>
    <string>-c</string>
    <string>curl -sS -m 1750 -H "Authorization: Bearer $(grep '^CRON_SECRET=' /Users/richardwhite/berwilson-platform/.env.local | cut -d= -f2-)" http://localhost:3000/api/cron/pepper-reply</string>
  </array>
  <!--
    Every 15 minutes, 07:30-18:00 on weekdays — Pepper answering a question you
    emailed her.

    ⚠ UNLIKE THE NUDGE IT SHARES THIS CADENCE WITH, THIS ONE DOES CALL THE
    MODEL — one agent turn per question, up to twelve tool rounds each. LM
    Studio serves ONE request at a time and nothing in this codebase queues, so
    the schedule is the concurrency control:

      - It starts at 07:30, AFTER the 05:30 chase / 06:20 curator / 06:30 brief
        / 06:45 text / 06:50 note / 07:00 digest block. Put it inside that hour
        and every morning job waits behind a question, or the question waits
        behind all of them and arrives an hour late.
      - MAX_PER_MEMBER caps the work per tick at three per person, so a flurry
        of replies cannot hold the GPU for an hour.
      - The timeout is 1750s against the route's own maxDuration of 1800: the
        curl must give up BEFORE the route does, or a run that actually
        completed is recorded as a failure.

    WHY 15 MINUTES. It is a reply to an email, so minutes matter and seconds do
    not; and the poll is cheap — one Gmail thread read per message Pepper has
    sent in the last 48 hours, which is a handful, not a mailbox search.

    IDEMPOTENCY IS THE GMAIL THREAD ITSELF, which is why there is no latch
    table. A question is only picked up when the LAST message on the thread is
    from the asker; the moment Pepper replies, the last message is hers and the
    thread drops out on its own. A send that succeeded cannot be answered twice
    even if the logging afterwards failed.
  -->
